lines+=['',f"Latest global route, no extra associative memory: **{diagnostic('ordinary'):.2f}%**. Historical-route oracle on these fresh backbones: **{diagnostic('oracle'):.2f}%**. The oracle requires the true digit pair and externally saved snapshots; automatic readouts above receive neither. A composed route can differ from every snapshot, so snapshot oracle is a reference, not a mathematical upper bound.",'',
'All methods predict among ten digits. Five seed means are shown; forward/reverse orders are paired within seed. The direct classifier is effectively deterministic under fixed image features and accumulated class sufficient statistics; its near-zero seed SD is expected, not five independently learned feature representations.','', '![Accuracy](accuracy.png)','',
'## What was learned online?','',
'1. A 25-neuron network with four branches per neuron learns each digit pair using factual cross-entropy and gradient-estimated usefulness. Training is unchanged by the association modules.','2. Before each gradient update, fixed features of the current images are associated with the branches currently selected by the network. Neither future snapshots nor a teacher-selected historical route is available.','3. At inference, each local branch compares the current features with its accumulated feature description. The best matching branch wins independently at each neuron; optional learned ring connections exchange three rounds of compatibility messages.','4. The selected responses feed the unchanged final ten-class classifier. No external route archive constrains the selection.','',
'Image keys have 64 dimensions: 63 values from fixed 7×9 average pooling, normalized and concatenated with a 0.2 bias before renormalization. There is no frozen learned encoder or extra pretrained model. Stable keys avoid the moving-feature problem seen in earlier work, but may limit recognition quality.','',
'Local memory stores explicit per-branch sums and counts. Validation selects cumulative updates or decay 0.995 on each update to an active branch. Dormant branches do not decay. This is activity-dependent fading, not a globally ticking short-term memory. The backbone separately retains fading usefulness scores (EMA 0.95). These associative arrays are additional memory; claiming that nothing extra is stored would be incorrect.','',
'## Controls and interpretation','',
'- The shared memories learn 96 or 144 aggregate input prototypes tagged with the branch pattern observed at write time. They store explicit associations/configurations, but no individual replay examples or externally supplied phase-end routes. The 96-slot control is close in size to local memory.','- Global ridge routing learns the same current-branch targets, using shared input covariance and cross-statistics. It tests whether a stronger centralized associative learner retrieves better. It is not a local branch-learning rule.','- The direct ridge classifier uses the same input keys and current training labels, with no branch network at deployment. It is a strong predictive control, although its class targets differ from branch-activity targets.','- No matched ordinary-network associative sidecar was tested here. Superiority to shared branch retrieval, if present, would not by itself establish an anatomical dendrite advantage.','',f"Local minus shared96: {diff('local','central96')}.",f"Communication minus local: {diff('local_communication','local')}.",f"Global ridge routing minus local: {diff('branch_rls','local')}.",'',
'These are descriptive intervals over five independent seeds, averaging orders first. They are not adjusted for multiple comparisons or evidence of generalization beyond this small dataset. Results must be read alongside the stronger direct-classifier baseline.','',
'## Selected settings','', '| Method | Validation-selected settings |','|---|---|']
